as we Mark Veterans Day and celebrate  those who have served and sacrificed we  are taking a look at a group with a  mission to connect veterans through  adventures in mountain biking the dirt  therapy project is a non-profit it  started by a retired marine and it aims  to restore the unique sense of  camaraderie that former service members  often miss and help some heal from the  wounds of War Omar villafranca caught up  with these Weekend Warriors in the Texas  Hill Country  all downhill from there  Army veteran Travis strong is no  stranger to heart pumping action from  skydiving to skiing  to surfing strong says he's always been  a bit of a daredevil but after surviving  a life-threatening Battlefield injury he  now just wants to show himself and  others that anything is possible  November 27th of 2006 was when it  actually happened we were just doing a  night Mission it kind of started off  really weird quiet strong was on his  second deployment to Baghdad when a  roadside bomb tore through the armored  vehicle he was riding in it took my  right leg off instantly and my left leg  was kind of really mangled but I didn't  feel any pain I didn't know I was even  hit at that point in time and then  everything just started to go black  I can feel myself just like draining out  like my life's just draining and I'm  like oh I'm I'm gonna die like I'm dead  doctors at a field Hospital were able to  save his life but not his legs at that  time I was just trying to think of what  my life would be like without legs and  just trying to figure out what can and  what I can I do you know the cans now  far outweigh the cannots strong even  hopes to Summit tanzania's Mount  Kilimanjaro next year but today he faces  a slightly smaller Peak hitting the  trails on this adaptive suspension trike  so this is gonna be your first time  coming down the mountains yes what do  you expected  um just have fun go down the trike was  loaned to him by a non-profit started by  another combat veteran Jonathan Hagerman  I grew up in a military family my dad  was Air Force and so it was always kind  of something that I knew I wanted to do  after four years of service including a  deployment to Afghanistan Hagerman  returned to civilian life and went back  to school what were you missing after  you left the Marines man that's a tough  question you build these bonds and these  friendships that you can't make anywhere  else for four years I had been on active  duty every single day I woke up I knew  what I was going to wear I knew where I  was allowed to go and then you separate  and it's like oh go do whatever you want  what is it that I want to do after a few  months of seeing the stress relieving  benefits of this Outlet on Wheels  Hagerman started the dirt therapy  project the mission helping other  veterans heal and connect through biking  the organization's first annual Veterans  Day event brought 150 veterans together  in Texas including strong and his  girlfriend Brittany a two-year Army vet  and competitive biker who couldn't wait  to hit the downhill dirt would you what  are you expecting on this mountain  he'll probably do stuff where we're all  going to go what the heck just happened  that's him so the brakes are not going  to get any use with him no probably not  [Applause]  when you're racing or when you're going  down a hill does it give you something  that you can't get anywhere else  a sense of freedom because pretty much  you're equal to everybody up there on  the mountain you can pretty much do  anything that they can do  so it's so sick this this helps me as  much as it helps anybody who comes out  to ride with us I'm becoming a better  version of myself  alongside other folks who are doing the  same thing I like to lead by example and  show what's possible instead of telling  other people hey do this when you're  doing it too and they can look at you  and go well I've got no excuse now for  CBS mornings I'm Omar villafranca in  Burnet Texas  well I take it back Mr strong and Mr  Hagerman when I said that looks scary  when you hear what they've been through  they could say Gail we have seen scary  that is not scary Bravo to them that  they could do it and I love it he said  you know I can do anything that anybody  else can do while I'm up here yes he  might be limited physically but he's  Limitless he's Limitless he's doing a  lot more than everyone else is doing I  see why they call it dirt therapy  totally get it yeah you want to do it  sometime soon no but we're cheering them  on okay moving on all right
 

